WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.849
Logan is arguably the best X-Men movie yet,
liberally borrowing elements from the popular

00:00:05.849 --> 00:00:07.750
Old Man Logan comics arc.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:12.269
That story was packed with references from
the Marvel Universe, and since Fox doesn't

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:16.680
have the rights to most of those characters,
it might seem safe to assume Logan doesn't

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.620
have many Easter eggs of its own.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:19.680
But you'd be wrong, bub.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.540
Here's a spoiler-filled look at some Logan
Easter eggs you totally missed.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.970
X-Men #132

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.280
One of Logan's key plot points comes from
the old issues of X-Men comics that Laura

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.719
carries around with her, which supposedly
contain the coordinates for the secret mutant

00:00:35.719 --> 00:00:37.030
haven Eden.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.550
One of these issues is #132.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.239
While the comic they show is a prop made for
the film, the decision to use #132 as the

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:46.449
issue number isn't random at all.

00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:52.670
In the real world, X-Men #132 marks the first
appearance of the cyborg Donald Pierce — one

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:56.899
of the film's main bad guys, who battled Wolverine
in that very issue.

00:00:56.899 --> 00:00:57.960
So meta!

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.809
Fat Albert

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:04.820
The evil Wolverine clone X-24 isn't in the
comics, but he seems to be based on a real

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.040
character with the slightly less scary name
of Albert.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.390
Albert was a robotic version of Wolverine
built by Donald Pierce as part of a weird

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.770
plan to kill Wolverine using an android bomb
disguised as a little girl.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:21.070
It didn't work, but at least Albert acquitted
himself a little better in the movies.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:24.370
Rictor, Rebecca, Charlotte, Bobby, Delilah

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:29.680
Just as Laura was created using Wolverine's
DNA, so too were her friends apparently created

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.140
using the DNA of other mutants.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.789
One of the kids, for instance, has Iceman's
freezing power, while another has Pyro's fire

00:01:36.789 --> 00:01:37.789
power.

00:01:37.789 --> 00:01:42.820
Logan also finds in the case files a reference
to Christopher Bradley, who appeared in X-Men

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.490
Origins: Wolverine as part of Team X.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:50.280
He was the mutant who could control electricity,
just like the kid who electrifies the Jeep

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.280
at the end.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.430
And finally, the leader of the kids, Rictor,
is a member of X-Force in the comics.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.829
Alkali Transigen

00:01:57.829 --> 00:02:03.299
The evil corporation breeding mutant slaves,
Alkali Transigen, got its really awkward name

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:07.950
from the comics: the secret facility where
Wolverine had the adamantium bonded to his

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.630
bones by William Stryker was actually located
at Alkali Lake, which was seen in several

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.849
previous X-Men movies.

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:16.849
The Reavers

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:21.750
Alkali Transigen isn't just making mutant
slaves, they're also really busy enhancing

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:26.620
their private army of human soldiers with
mechanical arms, legs, and other cybernetic

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:27.620
doodads.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.720
At one point in the movie, this gang of cyborg
mercenaries is referred to as the Reavers,

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.380
which is fitting, because in the comics, the
Reavers are an army of cyborgs financed and

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.760
commanded by — that's right — Donald Pierce.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:41.760
Knick-knacks

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.099
Considering how eagerly Logan seems to want
to forget his past, he carries an awful lot

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:47.290
of mementos.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:52.239
Not only is he toting around Chekov's adamantium
bullet, he also has a samurai sword on the

00:02:52.239 --> 00:02:55.090
wall of his shack as a nod to his time spent
in Japan.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:59.480
And he apparently bought his dog tags in bulk
at some point, because he seems to have an

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.239
endless supply of the things just to stare
at.

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:06.990
It just wouldn't be a Wolverine movie without
him staring at those dog tags.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.090
Greenwood Cemetery

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:12.330
At the beginning of the film, Logan is making
ends meet as a limo driver.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.620
He ends up bringing some random people to
a cemetery, where he has his first encounter

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.730
with Laura.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:22.739
But that's not just any burial ground: Greenwood
Cemetery has appeared in Marvel Comics for

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:27.220
years, in titles ranging from Fantastic Four
to The New Warriors.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.329
Patrick Stewart's childhood memories

00:03:29.329 --> 00:03:34.819
During a key bonding scene in which Professor
X and Laura watch the classic 1953 western

00:03:34.819 --> 00:03:39.950
Shane, Xavier tells her he saw the movie in
the Essoldo Cinema when he was just a little

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:40.950
kid himself.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:45.180
That may have actually been a true story,
because Essoldo was a chain of theaters that

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:51.150
operated in Northern England during the 1940s
and 1950s when Patrick Stewart was growing

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:52.610
up.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.159
"Fascinating, absolutely fascinating!"

00:03:56.159 --> 00:03:58.360
Thanks for watching!

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.099
